The code in this repository is aimed at various OEIS sequences having to
do with finding maximum-length arithmetic progressions of numbers that
share particular properties, such as having the same number of divisors.

The bulk of the C code is aimed specifically at finding values D(n, k)
for A292580, the least integer v such that tau(v + i) = n for all
0 <= i < k.

The source code is available on GitHub at:
  https://github.com/hvds/divrep

Further documentation is on the project wiki:
  https://github.com/hvds/divrep/wiki

WINDOWS
=======

Ready-built Windows executables of pcoul are available from the
releases page:
  https://github.com/hvds/divrep/releases

Each release has two zip files, one for Windows 10/11 and one that also
runs on Windows 7, each containing pcoul.exe and the DLLs it needs.
Extract all the files into the same directory, and run pcoul.exe from
there.

These are built by GitHub Actions; the steps used are in the files
under .github/workflows/. To build on Windows yourself, install Cygwin
with the packages those files list, and follow the same steps.

BUILD
=====

The code requires gcc, the GNU multi-precision library "libgmp", and
the code from Dana Jacobsen's "Math-Prime-Util-GMP" (MPUGMP), available
from:
  https://github.com/danaj/Math-Prime-Util-GMP

The Makefile expects a specific commit of MPUGMP, given by the variable
MPUGMP_VER. The current default is 39982f872b, the v0.54 release. Using
that, you need only tell the Makefile where to find the MPUGMP source:
  git clone https://github.com/danaj/Math-Prime-Util-GMP
  (cd Math-Prime-Util-GMP && git checkout v0.54)
  make MPUGMP=/path/to/Math-Prime-Util-GMP pcoul

To build against one of the other MPUGMP commits listed in the
Makefile, set MPUGMP_VER to match it: it decides which MPUGMP source
files are needed, and a mismatch typically shows up as link errors.

If your libgmp header file (gmp.h) is not in the standard include
paths, you will also need to modify the Makefile to include the
appropriate path.

There is no installation process: it is intended that the code should
be built and run in place.

The 'make' targets are:
pcoul, dpcoul: search for D(n, k), optimized/unoptimized for debugging
pcaul, dpcaul: equivalent to pcoul for A165498/A165499
pcrul, dpcrul: equivalent to pcoul for A309981
test, dtest: run the tests in t/ on the optimized/debugging programs;
  the soak tests among them run only if asked for, see the Makefile

Build-time options are set as variables on the make command line, or in
the environment, like:
  make PORTABLE=1 pcoul

The current build options are:

PORTABLE: build for a generic CPU. By default the code is compiled
for the CPU it is built on (-march=native), and may not run on other
machines. The Windows release builds use this option.

VERBOSE: print verbose information about factorization timings.

TRY_HARDER: also try slower factorization tests before giving up. This
adds 10 more ECM trials, the first taking about 15 minutes and each
subsequent one doubling the time. Generally this isn't worthwhile: if we
didn't find a factor before these tests, ECM probably won't find it.

DEBUG_ALL: print to the log file every number tested by linear search
(without a fixed square). Will fail if run without a log file.

SQONLY: skip any walk_v() call unless at least one value is forced
to be a square. Temporary facility to allow faster rechecking if bugs
are found in squares-handling code.

RUN
===

The standard way to run the program is:
  pcoul [options] <n> <k>

This searches for D(n, k) (equivalently T(n/2, k) in the terminology
of A292580). <n> and <k> come after all the options. On a Unix-like
system you will usually invoke it as "./pcoul"; on Windows as "pcoul"
or "pcoul.exe".

The one required option is "-x", giving the range to search: "-x<max>"
searches 0 < v <= max, "-x<min>:<max>" searches min < v <= max.
Numbers can be given in exponential notation without a decimal point,
so "-x13e6" means "-x13000000".

Each option takes its value directly after the option letter, with no
space. The full list of options is on the wiki page:
  https://github.com/hvds/divrep/wiki/pcoul-options

Output lines start with a 3-digit code. The most important are:
  202 Candidate <v>       a solution v has been found
  200 f(n, k) = <v>       the best solution found by the whole run
  305 ...                 progress, in the log file: the batch and
                          allocations being worked on
On the terminal, a progress line in the same form (without the code)
is by default shown every second, overwriting the previous one.

Examples:

  ./pcoul -x1e12 12 5

Searches for D(12, 5) up to 10^12. Each time a solution is found, it
is reported, and the search continues looking only for smaller ones.
This takes a fraction of a second, ending with "200 f(12, 5) = 204323":
since the search covers everything from 0 up to the maximum, this is
a proof that D(12, 5) = 204323.

  ./pcoul -rD12-9.log -x15724736975643 -f5 -W3e3 -g8 12 9

Searches for D(12, 9), writing progress to the log file "D12-9.log"
(by default at least once every 600 seconds; "-Lf60" would make it
every 60 seconds). If the run is interrupted, running the same command
again reads the log and continues from the last point it recorded. The
options "-f5 -W3e3 -g8" change how the search is done, but not what it
covers; choosing them well can make a big difference to the run time.
With these values it takes about 30 seconds on a modern machine. The
wiki page "D(n,k) calculation" explains what they do, and gives this
as a worked example of choosing them.

  ./pcoul -x1e20 -p50 24 7

"-p50" restricts the search to allocating primes up to 50, which is
usually a much faster way to find a first solution when nothing is
known. This finds 38577578523 in a few seconds. Because the search was
restricted, the final "200" line here shows only that D(24, 7) is at
most that value; a full run with "-x38577578523" would be needed to
show that nothing smaller exists.

  ./pcoul -Ls0 -a -x1e12 12 5 >batches
  ./pcoul -b3 -x1e12 12 5

The search works through "batches", each a way of placing the powers
of the small primes (those up to k) across the k values. The first
command lists the batches without searching them; "-Ls0" stops progress
lines from getting mixed into the output. A line of the list such as:
  203 b3: 2^5 . 2 . 2^2 [aq=64] (0.00s)
shows batch 3: v exactly divisible by 2^5, v + 2 by 2, and v + 4 by
2^2, with LCM 64. A batch with a larger LCM is usually quicker to
search. The second command searches only batch 3; since that is only
part of the search, its result is again only an upper bound. Running
every batch in this way covers the same ground as a single run.

